This tutorial completes [Workflow A](/en/usages/workflows/#workflow-a). On the
morning of 13 September, the pair checks Line 1 together. The snap traps
and pitfalls share a line and a team, but each cataloger prepares and records
the specimens they sort, on their own device.

Enter each specimen as in [Basic tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/basic/specimens/),
choosing the event, method, and collector from the shared event. For every
record, copy the capture date to `Collection date` and `Prep. date`, and add
two parts: `Whole body` in `Formalin` (`AL` on paper) and `Liver` in `ETOH`
with the tissue number as `Tissue ID`.

{% aside type="tip" title="Each cataloger keeps their own narrative" %}
In typical fieldwork, each cataloger keeps their own field journal on their own
device, written from what they saw and did. This series skips narratives.
Follow [Basic tutorial 6](/en/tutorials/basic/narrative/) to write one.
{% /aside %}

## 1. Heru catalogs the snap-trap catch

On the iPad, Heru catalogs the snap-trap catch from `TJHH-L1`, with himself
as `Collector`. His field IDs continue from `HH445`, so they match the paper
catalog:

| Field ID | `Taxon` | TTL–TL–HF–Ear, weight | `Sex` | `Life stage` | `Tissue ID` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `HH445` | *Mus crociduroides* | 205–103–25–15, 26 g | `Male`, testes scrotal | `Adult` | `Z26239` |
| `HH446` | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | 232–118–28–18, 51 g | `Female` | `Subadult` | `Z26241` |
| `HH447` | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | 218–109–29–18, 40 g | `Female` | `Subadult` | `Z26240` |
| `HH448` | *Niviventer cremoriventer* | 335–198–33–19, 63 g | `Male` | `Adult` | `Z26242` |
| `HH449` | *Niviventer cremoriventer* | 295–178–30–29, 47 g | `Female` | `Adult` | `Z26243` |

All five were captured at `07:00`, the team's standard time for overnight
captures. On paper, `449` is written over `448`, and the ear reads `29` mm,
which is long for this species. Enter both as written and add to `HH449`'s
`Remarks`: `Paper form: field number written over 448. Ear 29 mm as written.`

## 2. Bima catalogs the pitfall catch

On the iPhone, Bima catalogs the pitfall catch from `TJHH-P1`, with himself
as `Cataloger` and `Collector`:

| Field ID | Paper number | `Taxon` | Capture time | TTL–TL–HF–Ear, weight | `Sex` | `Tissue ID` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `BS1` | HH 451 | *Crocidura neglecta* | `07:00` | 109–44–12–6, 4.3 g | `Female` | `Z26245` |
| `BS2` | HH 452 | *Crocidura neglecta* | `07:00` | 116–50–11–6, 4.1 g | `Male` | `Z26246` |
| `BS3` | HH 453 | *Crocidura beccarii* | Empty | 150–75–17–8, 7 g | `Male` | `Z26247` |
| `BS4` | HH 454 | *Crocidura beccarii* | `07:00` | 150–71–16–9, 7.8 g | `Male` | `Z26248` |

All four are adults. `BS3` has no capture time on paper, so leave it empty.

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-bs1-capture.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Capture Records on an iPhone for BS1: site TJHH-P1, event TJHH-P1-2014-09-12, captured 13 September 2014 at 7:00 AM, method Pitfall, coordinate TJHH-PM, collector Bima Sampel"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

Because each cataloger has their own field-ID series, the two catalogs cannot
produce the same field ID, even though both devices work the same events.

